From c4cc278e3975059db6a65952dfb562db3f1e64b8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 12:56:10 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat(inventory): translate server-authoritative Bedrock
 requests
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Port inventory actions and crafting data onto the existing container tracker instead of the parallel PR 276 container hierarchy. Track predicted changes by request ID, apply server responses to the owning container, and restore snapshots after rejected requests. Include focused response and routing tests.

Decode crafting recipe ingredients with the protocol 2193 CraftingData layout: a presence variant, string descriptor subtype, and signed varint count. The auto-crafting request uses a different numeric descriptor layout. Read shaped and shapeless recipes plus their optional unlocking requirements without desynchronizing login packets.

Pin inventory action, text origin, container, and inventory source values to Mojang beta protocol 2193. The upstream generated snapshots contain later enum renumbering; using them would send incorrect action and slot IDs. Verify the emitted bytes in codec tests.

Open moving storage vehicles through their entity container IDs and display their vehicle titles. Close unsupported chemistry screens on Bedrock so they cannot lock later inventory actions. Support creative edits across player inventory slots and translate drag distribution and double-click collection.

Recover unanswered requests by reopening the player inventory for authoritative contents and cursor state. Disconnect with an explicit error if recovery also times out, since stale stack network IDs would make further clicks unsafe.

Accept hotbar response slots by their Bedrock backing index before mapping them to Java menu slots. The old ownership check rejected slots 0 through 8, losing server corrections and stack network IDs after inventory actions. Clear stale enchantment options and validate the input, lapis, and network IDs before requesting an enchantment.

Keep Cereal’s union discriminator before the embedded action type byte. For actions after Create, the embedded legacy ID is two greater because Cereal omits two obsolete container actions. Bedrock rejected crafting output when both IDs were written as 10.

Keep FullContainerName's presence byte and optional dynamic ID. Although the published protocol 2193 schema shows a fixed-width ID, the live 1.26.51.1 server sends the shorter encoding; using four bytes consumed the following storage item in InventoryContent.

Encode stack request slot IDs as little-endian 32-bit integers and keep optional container dynamic IDs. The versioned Cloudburst implementation for protocol 2193 agrees, while the public beta schema lists a different Cereal variant. The earlier wire format reached server-side slot validation; tagged variable integers caused packet parser rejection.

Queue a bounded number of clicks while a request is pending, then drain them against updated network IDs after each response. Discard queued screen actions when their screen closes. Preserve independent player-inventory writes across normal close acknowledgements; recovery discards all queued actions.

Include the enchanted result item in each enchantment request, matching the native action sequence. Preserve every server-provided enchantment on that result so Bedrock can validate options with multiple effects.

Encode enchantment craft results with the request-specific named-item descriptor, including user data. The ordinary inventory item type starts with a numeric item ID and made Bedrock reject the request as malformed.

Write both the Cereal union variant and its embedded item descriptor type. Omitting the second marker made Bedrock parse the item name length as an enum and disconnect.

Classify actor-backed container opens using the target entity ID. Bedrock uses generic CONTAINER for storage vehicles; checking its reported position as a chest block closed the Java screen on the next inventory tick.

Match the native enchant request with result item data, input and material consumption, and output placement. Use the effective Java game mode when deciding whether to consume lapis: Bedrock Default can inherit survival from the level, and omitting lapis then makes the server reject the request.

Decode creative item user data, route creative armor and offhand slots, accept their authoritative response slots, and replay clicks made before the player inventory opens. These corrections belong to the inventory flow introduced here.

Forward Java's mounted OPEN_INVENTORY command to the Bedrock Interact action with the mount runtime ID. The Java client sends this command when opening a storage vehicle while riding it; leaving it unhandled disconnects the client before Bedrock can open the container. Use the player runtime ID for an unmounted inventory request.

Capture immutable predicted slot identities when sending each authoritative request. Inventory-open contents can arrive before its successful response and overwrite a predicted item. Apply successful response counts and network IDs to the captured identity, while preserving server item metadata when an authoritative slot update already carries that response ID. Keep failure rollback revision checks separate. Canonicalize player-window snapshots to their shared physical inventory and retain sparse wire-slot mappings when copying containers.

Let the entity interaction transaction open unmounted storage vehicles. An extra OpenInventory Interact request suppresses the server container-open response on 1.26.51.1. Reconcile final custom names from successful stack responses, but preserve item damage because Bedrock reports a durability correction rather than an absolute damage value.

Open plain horse saddle and armor screens through Java's mount menu and route those slots through Bedrock HorseEquipContainer. Keep unsupported mount types closed until their layouts are verified. Assert the OpenInventory wire value accepted by the 1.26.51.1 server in the inventory interaction tests.

Expose Bedrock lecterns through the Java lectern menu using the block entity book and page state. Convert Bedrock two-page spreads to Java page buttons, derive the page count from the book when the block entity omits it, and send a lectern update only when the Bedrock spread changes. Schedule initial content after the menu opens and use a same-page update when Java closes it. Leave take-book disabled until its current Bedrock request semantics are verified.

Use the Bedrock beacon block entity Levels value for Java menu power, and refresh the open menu when block entity data changes. The container-open packet does not carry a strength field; reading server metadata also respects conditions beyond the local block palette.

Order hotbar drop actions as the container debit followed by the world
credit. Geyser recognizes this two-action normal transaction in that
order; the reversed sequence was ignored. Preserve single-item and
whole-stack counts without changing the tracked held stack.

Verified against protocol 2193 and Geyser 2.11.3's
BedrockInventoryTransactionTranslator at
https://github.com/GeyserMC/Geyser/blob/63a4e2b79b12f0d138777d5fd80176a112b4bd72/core/src/main/java/org/geysermc/geyser/translator/protocol/bedrock/BedrockInventoryTransactionTranslator.java
A focused transaction test checks action ordering and count conservation.

Accept the inventory, hotbar, and combined container aliases when reconciling
stack responses. Geyser returns hotbar names even for requests using the
combined name; ignoring those responses loses authoritative stack network
IDs. Validate slot bounds and reject dynamic container IDs as before.

Separate inventory-content indices from request slots at the container boundary. Protocol 2193 offhand contents occupy index zero, while requests and responses use equipment slot one. Translate both directions and preserve source and destination network IDs across cursor moves, swaps, quick moves, and drops. The mapping matches Geyser 2.11.3 PlayerInventoryTranslator at 63a4e2b79b12f0d138777d5fd80176a112b4bd72. Native Bedrock 1.26.51.1 captures through official Geyser build 1247 confirmed the offhand slot and network-ID flow on October 1, 2026.

Use authoritative requests for hand swaps when negotiated by START_GAME. Queue outside-screen swaps behind a player inventory open and close that temporary inventory after acknowledgments finish, including delayed opens after a queued action expires. Suppress equipment echoes while predicting clicks so servers receive their own confirmed stack IDs. Classify queued screen interactions separately so later inventory features can retain independent writes when a screen closes.

Allocate legacy inventory transaction IDs with a separate negative even counter. Static analysis of the matching Bedrock 1.26.51.1 executable confirms allocation starts at -4 and decreases by two; item-stack requests use the independent odd sequence. Keep native captures and disassembly private.

Process temporary player inventory CLOSE acknowledgements without forwarding another Java screen close. Java owns its creative and player screen; forwarding the native acknowledgement prematurely closes it and causes subsequent inputs to affect the wrong UI. Keep genuine server-initiated closures intact.

Write stonecutter ingredients as typed HolderSet fields instead of raw
VarInts. Both encodings produce the same Java wire bytes, but ViaBackwards
reads HolderSet objects while translating UPDATE_RECIPES for older Java
clients. Raw fields disconnected Java 26.2 clients during Geyser login.
The regression test covers typed translation reads and direct wire bytes.

Use the shared Java slot resolver for pickup clicks. Small external
screens otherwise remap the first player inventory cells into the
player crafting or armor slots after selecting the inventory container.
A native 1.26.51.1 ViaProxy merchant test exposed the rejected placement;
keep all click modes on the same screen-relative slot mapping.

Refresh full item data after taking crafted output. ItemStackResponse supplies
counts, names and stack network IDs, but omits server-generated tags such as
map UUIDs and enchantments. An InventoryMismatch transaction asks the server
for its complete player inventory, cursor, armor and offhand state without
closing the workstation. Hold queued clicks until all snapshots arrive and
crafted destinations match the acknowledged IDs and counts. Reuse inventory
recovery if this refresh does not complete.

Native Bedrock 1.26.51.1 build 51061372, protocol 2193, confirms that mismatch
requests return all four inventory snapshots. Correlation tests cover map
identity, stale IDs and amounts, multiple destinations, inventory aliases,
and a later snapshot invalidating an earlier slot update. Raw probes stay
private. The target packet schema is documented at
https://mojang.github.io/bedrock-protocol-docs/1.26.51/packets/inventory-transaction-packet/

Request an InventoryMismatch refresh when timed-out requests enter recovery.
Reopening the inventory alone does not guarantee fresh cursor and equipment
snapshots. Use the same refresh transaction already verified against native
Bedrock 1.26.51.1 for crafted metadata, and retain the authoritative recovery
barrier before allowing more predictions.

Install complete inventory snapshots before notifying slot observers, and
notify only changed stacks using full Bedrock item equality. Counts and
stack network IDs remain authoritative. Repeated strict-BDS food updates
send a release transaction followed by unchanged inventory and offhand
snapshots; the old callback loop emits duplicate equipment packets and
exposes partially replaced contents to recipe observers.

Protocol-2193 MobEquipment describes changes to held slots or contents:
https://mojang.github.io/bedrock-protocol-docs/1.26.51/packets/mob-equipment-packet/
Keep real changes, including renewed network IDs, while suppressing
unchanged snapshot callbacks. Preserve single-slot update semantics for
existing in-place prediction. Tests exercise repeated snapshots, count
and ID changes, coherent callback contents, and array ownership. Native
item-use prediction and completion timing remain a separate requirement.

Handle UPDATE_EQUIP alongside CONTAINER_OPEN for horse, donkey and mule
screens without opening the same screen twice. Preserve native capacity,
content indices and request identities independently from Java menu slots.
Java retains a second equipment cell for donkeys and mules, so cargo slots
shift by one and player contents begin after the full Java slot array.
Use server equipment item and auxiliary-value restrictions for predictions
and quick moves. Preserve those slot mappings in rejection snapshots and
hide inaccessible cargo from unchested menus.

The protocol-2193 UPDATE_EQUIP schema defines byte container IDs, a signed
varint size, the actor unique ID and serialized data:
https://mojang.github.io/bedrock-protocol-docs/1.26.51/packets/update-equip-packet/
Geyser's mount opener sends size zero, while its chested translator maps
one native equipment slot and shifts cargo around Java's second slot:
https://github.com/GeyserMC/Geyser/blob/f66329d9d21b3c836edc014fbb9d8312fbe34285/core/src/main/java/org/geysermc/geyser/translator/inventory/horse/ChestedHorseInventoryTranslator.java
Use supported actor metadata and chest state instead of assuming that
UPDATE_EQUIP's size always describes native backing capacity.

An isolated headless official BDS 1.26.51.1, build 51061372, protocol 2193
probe confirms tamed mule capacity 16 before chest attachment, the CHESTED
flag after attachment, and Script API cargo items at indices 1 and 15.
It does not establish native screen-opening behavior or live Java request
parity. Unit tests cover packet registration and both opening orders,
equipment restrictions, hidden cells, all cargo/player mappings, native
network IDs, compact snapshots and rejection snapshot identity.
Keep proprietary captures private; full native, direct and ViaProxy mount
inventory comparisons remain required.
